		{"type":"text","text":"Highlights Code:\n- <mark style=\"background: #BBFABBA6;\">Descriptions/Setting</mark>\n- <mark style=\"background: #FFF3A3A6;\">Exposition</mark>\n- <mark style=\"background: #FFB86CA6;\">Characterization</mark>\n- <mark style=\"background: #ABF7F7A6;\">Quote</mark>\n- <mark style=\"background: #FF5582A6;\">Action</mark>\n- <mark style=\"background: #D2B3FFA6;\">Foreshadowing</mark>","id":"34b991185f7af6ef","x":-494,"y":-339,"width":250,"height":439},
		{"id":"00429451cc993dc4","type":"text","text":"Dialog:\n\n1. What do they keep steering the conversation towards?\n2. What are they avoiding?\n3. Interruptions\n4. Whad do they justify?","x":-63,"y":-592,"width":383,"height":312},
		{"id":"685d48282181f9b7","type":"text","text":"📌 Save this BEFORE you write another chapter.\n\nAfter more than a decade working in publishing, I’ve learned that one of the biggest mistakes aspiring authors make happens long before they write page one.\n\nThey spend months, sometimes YEARS, writing a manuscript without first determining whether their idea is strong enough to succeed in the market.\n\nAnd here’s something most aspiring authors don’t realize: agents and publishers are evaluating far more than your ability to write beautiful prose.\n\nHere are 7 questions I want you to be able to answer:\n\n1. WHO is your ideal reader? Can you identify exactly who’s going to buy your book and what they’re already reading?\n\n2. WHAT is your book about? Can you communicate your central premise in one compelling sentence?\n\n3. WHAT makes it different? Why would someone choose your book over the other titles in its genre?\n\n4. WHAT are the stakes? What does your protagonist stand to lose, and why should readers care?\n\n5. WHERE does it belong? Do you understand your genre, market positioning, and comparable titles?\n\n6. DOES your story deliver? Does your plot build toward a satisfying ending that feels both surprising and inevitable?\n\n7. WHY are YOU writing it? What unique perspective, experience, or creative vision makes this story yours to tell?\n\nHere’s what I want every aspiring author to understand:\n\nBeautiful writing cannot compensate for a weak premise, unclear positioning, or a story that fails to deliver on its promise.\n\nAnd the sooner you identify those weaknesses, the easier they are to fix.\n\nThe strongest books begin with a strong foundation. And that foundation starts BEFORE page one.\n\nWant to know how strong YOUR book idea really is?","x":-146,"y":-220,"width":550,"height":1420},
		{"id":"060620b8f8abb4e2","type":"text","text":"WRITING TIPS\n\n1. Write badly first.  \n    First drafts exist to exist. Quality comes from revision, not inspiration.\n    \n2. Be concrete, not abstract.  \n    Don’t write ‘sad’—write ‘she stared at the wall until it went dark.’\n    \n3. Cut 10–20% of your words.  \n    Almost every piece improves when you delete what’s unnecessary.\n    \n4. Prefer strong verbs over adjectives.  \n    “He slammed the door” beats “He closed the door angrily.”\n    \n5. Read your work out loud.  \n    If it sounds awkward, it is awkward.\n    \n6. One idea per paragraph.  \n    If a paragraph wanders, split it or delete the detour.\n    \n7. Show intention early.  \n    Let the reader know what the piece is about in the first few lines.\n    \n8. Imitate good writers—then stop.  \n    Copy their structure and rhythm to learn, but don’t trap yourself in their voice.\n    \n9. Enter scenes late; leave early.  \n    Start as close to the action as possible. Exit before things go stale.\n    \n10. Finish things.  \n    An imperfect finished piece beats a brilliant unfinished one every time.","x":480,"y":-184,"width":700,"height":824},
		{"id":"3ad05c86b13fab5d","x":1320,"y":-592,"width":700,"height":1712,"type":"text","text":"Most first-time authors make their biggest mistake before they write a single bad sentence.\n\nThey start writing before they actually understand the book they’re trying to write.\n\nIf I were starting over, I’d figure out these five things first:\n\n1. PREMISE Can I explain the book in one clear, compelling sentence?\n\nFor nonfiction: What is the central idea, problem, or transformation?\n\nFor fiction: Who wants what, what stands in their way, and why does it matter?\n\nIf the premise is muddy, the manuscript usually will be too.\n\n2. IDEAL READER Who is this book specifically for?\n\nI’d get painfully clear about the person picking it up, what they want from it, and why THIS book would matter to them.\n\n“Everyone” is not an ideal reader.\n\n3. GENRE + COMPS Where does this book belong in the market?\n\nI’d identify its exact genre or category and study successful comparable books. What expectations are they creating? What are readers responding to? Where does my book fit while still offering something distinct?\n\nYou need to understand the shelf before you try to earn your place on it.\n\n4. CORE CONFLICT What creates enough tension to carry an entire book?\n\nIn fiction, that’s the central problem driving your protagonist forward.\n\nIn nonfiction, it’s the gap between where your reader is now and where they desperately want to be, including what keeps getting in their way.\n\nWithout tension, you may have information or events. You don’t necessarily have a book.\n\n5. STRUCTURE Only then would I build the roadmap.\n\nI’d know how the book progresses, what each chapter needs to accomplish, and why every section earns its place.\n\nTHEN I’d write chapter one.\n\nBecause if I could give first-time-author Ashley one piece of advice, it would be this:\n\nI wouldn’t try to become a better writer first.\n\nI’d become clearer about the book I was trying to write.\n\nClarity saves an extraordinary amount of rewriting.\n\nSave these five steps before you start your book."},
		{"id":"b1f058be804ef606","x":2160,"y":-592,"width":712,"height":1312,"type":"text","text":"📌 Save this if you want your first draft to feel a whole lot easier.\n\nThe authors who move through the writing process with the most clarity tend to understand something early: a great book is easier to write when you build the right foundation first.\n\n1. They front-load the difficult decisions. They get clear on the premise, ideal reader, core conflict, structure, genre, and where the book is going before they accumulate thousands of words. That means when they sit down to write, they can actually write instead of trying to solve the entire book at the same time.\n\n2. They get professional support early. This is a huge differentiator. They don’t assume they have to struggle through an entire first draft alone before they’re “ready” for a book coach. They seek coaching, mentorship, and editorial guidance while the book is still being shaped, when feedback can save them enormous amounts of unnecessary rewriting later.\n\n3. They build accountability into the process. They establish a writing cadence, protect the time, set deadlines, and have someone helping them stay in motion. Even 15 focused minutes a day becomes powerful when it’s part of a consistent process.\n\nThat’s what I wish more aspiring authors understood about book coaching.\n\nYou don’t have to arrive with a finished manuscript. You can arrive with an idea, a few chapters, a messy outline, or a book you simply know you’re ready to take seriously.\n\nIn fact, getting the right guidance early can be one of the smartest decisions you make.\n\nBecause the goal isn’t simply to write more words.\n\nIt’s to know you’re building the right book as you write them."},
		{"id":"3558c5054984a693","x":-725,"y":128,"width":481,"height":592,"type":"text","text":"Possible Comps:\n\n- Thrice-Bound Fool\n- "}
